    "description": "Iran’s cabinet, constitutionally described as the Council of Ministers, coordinates executive policy under the president. Ministers require parliamentary confidence, and their administrative regulations must remain within the law. [s1]",
    "tags": ["Cabinet", "Council of Ministers", "Ministers", "Parliament", "Executive administration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:17:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:17:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Cabinet: Ministers, Confidence Votes and Decisions | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how Iran’s Council of Ministers is formed, how ministers answer to parliament, and how cabinet decisions differ from legislation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "formation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How a minister takes office",
        "content": "Under Article 133, the president appoints ministers and presents them to parliament for confidence votes. Law determines ministerial numbers and responsibilities; election of a new parliament does not itself require renewed confidence. The president heads the Council of Ministers and coordinates government policy. [s1]\n\nMinisters answer to both the president and parliament. The president may dismiss them, but replacements require parliamentary confidence. Changing half the cabinet after its confidence vote triggers a fresh government confidence vote. A ministry without a minister may have a presidentially appointed caretaker for up to three months. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "decisions-and-accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Collective decisions within legal limits",
        "content": "Article 138 authorizes administrative and implementing regulations, which must conform to law. Cabinet regulations and specified ministerial commission decisions are communicated to the parliamentary speaker for review of consistency with legislation. Ministers also bear responsibility for decisions approved collectively. [s1]\n\nThis distinguishes cabinet administration from legislation: a government regulation cannot legitimately substitute for a legal amendment. Confidence votes concern political accountability, while review of regulations concerns legal limits. These mechanisms address different questions and do not by themselves establish effective oversight. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-formation-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented formation episode",
        "content": "On 21 August 2024, parliament approved all nineteen ministers proposed by President Masoud Pezeshkian, according to Iran Press’s contemporaneous report. Its list covered portfolios ranging from education and health to petroleum, intelligence, foreign affairs and roads and urban development. The breadth of that list illustrates the cabinet’s administrative reach, but does not establish that every state institution falls under ministerial command. [s2]\n\nIndependent reporting by Associated Press described the appointments as a negotiated consensus and identified continuity with the preceding administration in several portfolios. Those observations concern the composition and politics of the initial team. They are not evidence that the same individuals remained in office at the editorial cutoff. [s3]\n\nWhen reading a cabinet announcement, distinguish nomination, parliamentary approval and subsequent tenure. Likewise, an approved ministerial program is an undertaking whose implementation needs separate evidence. The initial confidence vote is a documented institutional event; judging service delivery or policy effectiveness requires budgets, implementation records and outcomes. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "formation-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Ministerial formation and accountability",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  P[\"President\"] -->|\"Presents minister for confidence vote\"| A[\"Parliament\"]\n  A -->|\"Grants or withholds confidence\"| M[\"Proposed minister\"]\n  M -->|\"If approved, participates in\"| C[\"Council of Ministers\"]\n  P -->|\"Heads and coordinates\"| C\n  M -->|\"Answers for ministerial duties\"| P\n  M -->|\"Answers for ministerial duties\"| A\n  C -->|\"Communicates regulations for legal consistency review\"| S[\"Parliamentary speaker\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates approval of ministers, presidential coordination, ministerial accountability and review of regulations. It summarizes constitutional procedures rather than informal political bargaining. [s1]",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
